/* AFK Quest integrated cases: c01 to c18. Each case joins at least three modules.
   Every fact, dose and threshold is taken from the verified node content in content/*.js;
   the node named on each step is the one that teaches it. */
window.CASES = (window.CASES || []).concat([

{ id:"c01", title:"Spreading infection from 3.7 in a diabetic adult", areas:["BIO","OSX","PHA"],
  stem:"<p>A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes treated with insulin presents with three days of throbbing pain and a swelling below the left lower border of the mandible. 3.7 has deep caries and does not respond to cold. His temperature is 38.6 °C and his interincisal opening is 30 mm. He can swallow and breathe comfortably. His last HbA1c was 9.1%.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"The swelling lies below the lower border of the mandible. Which primary space has the infection from 3.7 entered?",
     answer:"Submandibular space", wrong:["Sublingual space","Buccal space","Submental space"],
     explain:"The apices of 3.7 and 3.8 lie below the mylohyoid attachment, so pus enters the submandibular space. Sublingual spread comes from apices above the mylohyoid line, such as the premolars and usually 3.6.",
     node:"m02-n06" },
   { q:"Which finding in this stem meets one of Hupp's referral triggers?",
     answer:"Temperature of 38.6 °C", wrong:["Interincisal opening of 30 mm","Three days of throbbing pain","Deep caries in a molar"],
     explain:"Hupp's temperature trigger is above 38.3 °C, and a compromised host is a trigger too. Trismus counts only when opening is severely limited, so an opening of 30 mm does not meet it; pain and caries are not triggers.",
     node:"m21-n04" },
   { q:"How should his HbA1c of 9.1% change the plan for the infection?",
     answer:"Treat now and expect slower healing", wrong:["Defer treatment until HbA1c is under 7.0%","Expect a hypoglycaemic collapse today","Give glucagon before any surgery"],
     explain:"HbA1c reports average glucose over two to three months, so it predicts healing and infection, not today's collapse. Acute infection is treated promptly whatever the number; only elective surgery is weighed against the value.",
     node:"m13-n06" },
   { reveal:"<p>The source is removed and the pus drained. He has systemic signs, so an antibiotic is added. He reports that penicillin once gave him hives.</p>",
     q:"Which oral regimen fits the ADA 2019 guideline for this allergy?",
     answer:"Azithromycin 500 mg day 1, then 250 mg for 4 days", wrong:["Cephalexin 500 mg four times daily for 3 to 7 days","Amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for 3-7 days","Metronidazole 500 mg alone three times daily"],
     explain:"Hives, angioedema or anaphylaxis with penicillin calls for azithromycin or clindamycin. Cephalexin is reserved for allergy without those features, and metronidazole covers only anaerobes, so it is an add-on, never a solo drug.",
     node:"m11-n05" },
   { reveal:"<p>While waiting for his prescription he becomes sweaty, tremulous and slightly confused. He took his insulin but skipped breakfast. His glucose is 3.1 mmol/L and he can swallow.</p>",
     q:"What is the correct immediate management?",
     answer:"15 g of oral glucose, recheck in 15 minutes", wrong:["Glucagon 1 mg intramuscularly before anything else","Lay him supine and give oxygen only","Epinephrine 0.5 mg into the thigh"],
     explain:"Below 4.0 mmol/L with symptoms in a patient who can swallow, give 15 g of fast carbohydrate and retest at 15 minutes. Glucagon is for the unconscious patient, who must receive nothing by mouth.",
     node:"m13-n02" },
   { reveal:"<p>Three days later he is no better.</p>",
     q:"What should you check first?",
     answer:"Whether the source was removed and the pus drained", wrong:["Whether a broader antibiotic should replace the first","Whether his HbA1c has already fallen since the first visit","Whether the antibiotic dose should simply be doubled now"],
     explain:"Re-evaluate within 3 days. An undrained collection or a retained source defeats any antibiotic, so check drainage first, then host defences, then the drug choice.",
     node:"m21-n04" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Anatomy placed the pus: an apex below the mylohyoid means the submandibular space. Surgery set the priorities (drain, remove the source, refer on Hupp's triggers), and endocrinology split today's risk (hypoglycaemia) from tomorrow's (healing, shown by HbA1c). Pharmacology chose the drug last, by allergy history.</p>" },

{ id:"c02", title:"Extractions for a patient on warfarin", areas:["BIO","PHA","OSX"],
  stem:"<p>A 68-year-old woman on long-term warfarin, target INR 2.0 to 3.0, needs 4.6 and 4.7 extracted because both are non-restorable. She takes no other medicines and has no other medical problems. She asks whether she should stop her warfarin for a few days first.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Warfarin acts by antagonising vitamin K. Which clotting factors does it depress?",
     answer:"Factors II, VII, IX and X", wrong:["Factors VIII, IX, XI and XII","Factors V, VIII and fibrinogen","Factors XI, XII and von Willebrand"],
     explain:"Factors II, VII, IX and X need vitamin K to become functional. PT/INR reports the extrinsic and common paths, which is why it monitors warfarin.",
     node:"m03-n06" },
   { q:"Which test result do you need before the extractions?",
     answer:"An INR within 24 hours, or 72 hours if stable", wrong:["An aPTT taken on the morning of the extractions","A platelet count taken at any point in the past year","A bleeding time measured at any time in the past week"],
     explain:"The INR measures warfarin's effect; check it ideally within 24 hours, or within 72 hours if the patient is stable. The aPTT monitors unfractionated heparin and haemophilia, not warfarin.",
     node:"m13-n05" },
   { reveal:"<p>Her INR this morning is 2.8.</p>",
     q:"What do you advise about the warfarin?",
     answer:"Continue it and control bleeding locally", wrong:["Stop it for five days and then extract","Halve the dose on the day before the surgery","Replace it with aspirin for one week"],
     explain:"At an INR of 3.5 or below, simple extractions proceed without stopping warfarin, using local measures. Stopping it invites thrombosis, which is the greater danger.",
     node:"m21-n05" },
   { q:"Which local adjunct is specifically suited to an anticoagulated patient?",
     answer:"Tranexamic acid 5% mouthwash", wrong:["Chlorhexidine 0.12% mouthwash","Hydrogen peroxide mouthwash","Povidone-iodine mouthwash"],
     explain:"Tranexamic acid, as a 5% mouthwash or soaked gauze, blocks plasmin activation so the clot is not dissolved; it is the rung of the haemostasis ladder for coagulopathy or anticoagulation. The other rinses are antiseptics, not haemostatics.",
     node:"m21-n03" },
   { q:"Which analgesic do you recommend after the extractions?",
     answer:"Acetaminophen 1000 mg every 6 hours", wrong:["Ibuprofen 400 mg every 6 hours","Aspirin 650 mg every 4 hours","Ibuprofen with aspirin as needed"],
     explain:"NSAIDs and aspirin add bleeding risk to an anticoagulated patient. Acetaminophen has little platelet effect; the healthy-adult daily maximum is 4 g. Sustained high doses can still raise the INR modestly, so keep the course short.",
     node:"m11-n04" },
   { reveal:"<p>Four days later the 4.7 socket is infected. A colleague wants to add metronidazole.</p>",
     q:"What is the main concern with metronidazole in this patient?",
     answer:"It raises the warfarin effect, so monitor INR", wrong:["It lowers the warfarin effect, so clots are more likely","It inactivates the tranexamic acid in the socket","It blocks acetaminophen and so worsens pain"],
     explain:"Metronidazole, fluconazole and miconazole raise warfarin's effect (metronidazole through CYP2C9 inhibition), so closer INR monitoring is arranged with the prescriber.",
     node:"m11-n08" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Physiology explains the drug (vitamin K-dependent factors), which explains the test (INR, not aPTT). Cardiology and surgery agree on the decision: keep the anticoagulant and control the socket locally. Pharmacology then guards the recovery: no NSAID, and watch for antibiotics that push the INR up.</p>" },

{ id:"c03", title:"Avulsed 1.1 in an 8-year-old", areas:["END","OSX","BIO","PHA"],
  stem:"<p>A mother phones at 4 pm: her 8-year-old son fell from his bicycle on the road 10 minutes ago and knocked out his upper right central incisor. She has found the tooth, which looks intact. He did not lose consciousness and has no other injuries. She can reach your office in about 30 minutes.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"What is the best instruction to give her on the phone?",
     answer:"Hold it by the crown, rinse it briefly, replant it", wrong:["Scrub the root clean and wrap it in a tissue","Keep it dry in a sealed bag and come in","Place it in a glass of tap water and come in"],
     explain:"Replant at once, holding the crown, after a brief rinse if dirty. If that is impossible, store it in milk, HBSS, saliva or saline; water is poor and drying kills PDL cells.",
     node:"m20-n06" },
   { reveal:"<p>She could not replant it, but put it in milk 10 minutes after the fall. They arrive 40 minutes later.</p>",
     q:"What do you do before replanting the tooth?",
     answer:"Rinse the root with saline and irrigate the socket gently", wrong:["Curette the socket walls back to fresh bone","Scrape the whole root surface clean with a sharp curette first","Leave the tooth dry on the tray until the socket stops bleeding"],
     explain:"With a dry time under 60 minutes and storage in a medium, rinse the root with saline, irrigate the clot away gently without curetting, and replant with light pressure. Curetting or scraping destroys the PDL cells you are trying to keep.",
     node:"m21-n06" },
   { q:"How is the replanted 1.1 stabilised?",
     answer:"Passive flexible splint for 2 weeks", wrong:["Rigid wire splint for 6 weeks","Flexible splint for 4 months","No splint, soft diet only"],
     explain:"IADT 2020 uses a passive flexible splint for 2 weeks after avulsion, because slight movement helps the PDL heal. A more rigid splint for about 4 weeks is kept for an accompanying alveolar or jaw fracture.",
     node:"m20-n06" },
   { q:"Why is root canal treatment not started within 2 weeks in this tooth?",
     answer:"At age 8 the apex of 1.1 is still open", wrong:["Replanted teeth never need endodontic treatment","The PDL must heal for six months beforehand","Calcium hydroxide is unsafe in children under 12"],
     explain:"The maxillary central incisor erupts at about 7 to 8 years and the root closes about two to three years after emergence. A wide apex can revascularise, so you monitor and treat at the first sign of necrosis.",
     node:"m06-n08" },
   { q:"Which systemic antibiotic suits him?",
     answer:"Amoxicillin, as tetracyclines are avoided in children", wrong:["Doxycycline, as it is the first choice in the IADT guideline","Metronidazole alone, as it covers all the oral organisms","No antibiotic, as the IADT guideline advises against one"],
     explain:"IADT recommends a systemic antibiotic after avulsion, though its value is questionable, with amoxicillin or penicillin first. Tetracyclines chelate calcium in forming teeth and bone, which is exactly why they are avoided in young children.",
     node:"m11-n05" },
   { reveal:"<p>His records show he is fully immunised; his last tetanus-containing dose was 4 years ago.</p>",
     q:"Does this road-contaminated wound need a tetanus booster?",
     answer:"No, as his last dose was under 5 years ago", wrong:["Yes, as any wound needs a booster after 1 year","Yes, plus tetanus immune globulin at once","No, as primary-age children never need one"],
     explain:"With 3 or more previous doses, a wound other than a clean minor one needs a booster if the last dose was 5 or more years ago. Immune globulin is added only for an unknown or incomplete history.",
     node:"m21-n06" },
   { reveal:"<p>At 3 months the radiograph shows bowl-shaped radiolucencies in the root and adjacent bone.</p>",
     q:"What does this mean, and what do you do?",
     answer:"Infection-related resorption: start canal treatment now", wrong:["Replacement resorption: plan decoronation of the tooth now","Normal healing: review again at 6 months","Internal resorption: remove the splint and wait for healing"],
     explain:"Bowl-shaped radiolucencies in root and bone mark infection-related resorption, a sign of an infected necrotic pulp in an immature tooth. Start root canal treatment with calcium hydroxide at once, because this resorption is very fast in children.",
     node:"m20-n07" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Every decision protected either the PDL (dry time, storage, no curetting, a flexible splint) or the pulp. Growth biology decided the pulp plan: an 8-year-old's 1.1 has an open apex, so you wait for revascularisation but act the moment infection-related resorption appears. Pharmacology and immunisation rules covered the wound itself.</p>" },

{ id:"c04", title:"Scaling for a patient with a prosthetic valve", areas:["PHA","BIO","PER"],
  stem:"<p>A 61-year-old man had his mitral valve replaced with a prosthetic valve 4 years ago. He has generalised calculus, bleeding on probing at 45% of sites and pockets up to 5 mm. Ten years ago penicillin gave him urticaria. He needs subgingival instrumentation.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Does AHA 2021 suggest endocarditis prophylaxis for his instrumentation?",
     answer:"Yes, because of the prosthetic valve", wrong:["No, as the valve is over 6 months old","No, as scaling is not a listed procedure","Only if his gingiva bleeds heavily"],
     explain:"A prosthetic valve is on the highest-risk list, and manipulation of gingival tissue qualifies. The 6-month limit applies to complete congenital repairs with prosthetic material, not to valve replacement.",
     node:"m13-n09" },
   { q:"Which oral organisms are the usual cause of endocarditis after dental bacteraemia?",
     answer:"Viridans streptococci", wrong:["Candida albicans","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Porphyromonas gingivalis"],
     explain:"Viridans streptococci, the alpha-haemolytic oral streptococci, enter the blood during daily life and dental treatment and stick to damaged or prosthetic valves.",
     node:"m04-n05" },
   { q:"Why is cephalexin 2 g unsuitable for him?",
     answer:"Urticaria with penicillin rules out cephalosporins", wrong:["Cephalexin was removed from AHA 2021 for all patients","Cephalexin cannot be given within 60 minutes","Cephalexin is reserved for children weighing under 45 kg"],
     explain:"Cephalosporins must not be given after anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria with a penicillin. Cephalexin 2 g remains an AHA option for other allergies; it was clindamycin that was removed in 2021.",
     node:"m13-n09" },
   { reveal:"<p>His cardiologist's letter notes a prolonged QTc interval.</p>",
     q:"Which single oral dose, 30 to 60 minutes before, is now the best choice?",
     answer:"Doxycycline 100 mg", wrong:["Azithromycin 500 mg","Amoxicillin 2 g","Doxycycline 20 mg"],
     explain:"Macrolides prolong the QT interval, so azithromycin is used cautiously here, and amoxicillin is excluded by the allergy. Doxycycline 100 mg is the AHA allergy option; 20 mg is the sub-antimicrobial periodontal dose.",
     node:"m11-n05" },
   { q:"Which instrumentation schedule best fits the evidence and limits repeated doses?",
     answer:"Full-mouth instrumentation within 24 hours", wrong:["Four quadrant visits on consecutive days","Four quadrant visits at weekly intervals","Supragingival scaling only, with no prophylaxis"],
     explain:"Quadrant-wise and full-mouth instrumentation within 24 hours give similar results. AHA advises allowing 10 days after a dose before the next procedure where possible, so short-interval serial visits are the worst option, and leaving subgingival biofilm untreated is not therapy.",
     node:"m19-n05" },
   { reveal:"<p>At the visit he admits he forgot to take the dose. You have already started.</p>",
     q:"What does AHA 2021 advise?",
     answer:"Give it up to 2 hours after the procedure", wrong:["Abandon the visit and rebook in 10 days","Give double the dose before continuing","Give nothing, as the window has passed"],
     explain:"If the dose is missed, it can be given up to 2 hours after the procedure.",
     node:"m13-n09" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Cardiology decided who qualifies (a prosthetic valve) and microbiology explained why (viridans streptococci on a foreign surface). The allergy history and the ECG then narrowed the drug: no cephalosporin after urticaria, caution with macrolides and a long QTc. Periodontal evidence shaped the schedule so fewer doses were needed.</p>" },

{ id:"c05", title:"Chest pain in the chair in an angina patient", areas:["BIO","PHA"],
  stem:"<p>A 64-year-old man with stable angina carries a nitroglycerin spray. His pressure before treatment is 138/86 mmHg. He is very anxious, and during preparation of 4.6 his pulse rises to 112 per minute. He then stops you, pale and sweating, with central chest pressure spreading to his left arm.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Why can his fast heart rate bring on angina?",
     answer:"Demand rises while diastolic filling time shortens", wrong:["Demand falls while coronary blood flow roughly doubles","Systolic coronary filling is cut in half","Tachycardia constricts the coronary veins"],
     explain:"The coronary arteries fill mainly during diastole, so a fast heart both needs more oxygen and gets less time to receive it. Narrowed arteries cannot meet that demand.",
     node:"m03-n01" },
   { q:"His systolic pressure is 128 mmHg. Before giving nitroglycerin, which question matters most?",
     answer:"Has he taken sildenafil or tadalafil?", wrong:["When did he last eat a meal?","Is he allergic to penicillin?","Did he take his statin today?"],
     explain:"Nitroglycerin is contraindicated after sildenafil or vardenafil within 24 hours or tadalafil within 48 hours: these drugs preserve cGMP, and adding a nitrate can cause profound hypotension.",
     node:"m13-n04" },
   { q:"He has taken none. How do you give the nitroglycerin?",
     answer:"One 0.4 mg spray under the tongue, every 5 min up to 3", wrong:["One 0.4 mg spray onto the cheek, every 15 min up to 5","Two 0.4 mg sprays under the tongue, once only","One 0.3 mg tablet chewed and swallowed, every 30 min as needed"],
     explain:"One 0.4 mg metered spray sublingually, repeated every 5 minutes for up to 3 doses, while systolic pressure stays at least 90 mmHg.",
     node:"m13-n04" },
   { reveal:"<p>Five minutes after the first spray the pain is no better, and he feels nauseated. His oxygen saturation is 97%.</p>",
     q:"What is the next step?",
     answer:"Call 9-1-1 and give 325 mg of chewed non-enteric-coated ASA", wrong:["Give a second spray and wait another 15 minutes before calling","Give high-flow oxygen by mask and then resume the restoration","Lay him flat and give 15 g of oral glucose"],
     explain:"Pain not better 5 minutes after the first dose, with features of MI, means calling emergency services. Give aspirin 162 to 325 mg chewed unless allergic or bleeding. Routine oxygen brings no benefit when saturation is normal.",
     node:"m13-n04" },
   { reveal:"<p>An MI is confirmed in hospital. Months later his physician agrees that elective care can resume.</p>",
     q:"What epinephrine limit applies to his future visits?",
     answer:"0.04 mg, about two cartridges of 1:100,000", wrong:["0.2 mg, about eleven cartridges of 1:100,000","0.04 mg, about eight cartridges of 1:200,000","No epinephrine at any dose, ever again"],
     explain:"The conventional ceiling with significant cardiovascular disease is 0.04 mg: two 1.8 mL cartridges of 1:100,000 or four 1.7 mL cartridges of 1:200,000. 0.2 mg is the healthy-patient ceiling. Good anaesthesia also limits his own adrenaline surge.",
     node:"m12-n02" },
   { q:"Which analgesic suits him after a later extraction?",
     answer:"Acetaminophen", wrong:["High-dose ibuprofen","High-dose diclofenac","Celecoxib"],
     explain:"After a recent MI, avoid NSAIDs and use acetaminophen. High-dose diclofenac and COX-2-selective drugs raised major vascular events by about a third in the 2013 CNT meta-analysis.",
     node:"m13-n05" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Cardiac physiology explains why fear in the chair produces ischaemia: more demand, less diastolic filling. The emergency algorithm turns on two checks (pressure and PDE-5 inhibitors) and one timer (5 minutes). After the infarct, anaesthesia and analgesic choices follow the same logic of limiting cardiac stress.</p>" },

{ id:"c06", title:"A white lesion in a heavy smoker", areas:["OMR","OSX","BIO","EBD"],
  stem:"<p>A 58-year-old man who has smoked for 30 pack-years and drinks heavily every day has a painless white plaque about 2 cm across on the right ventrolateral tongue. It does not wipe off with gauze, does not fade when stretched, and there is no sharp tooth or appliance next to it. He has no idea how long it has been there.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Which clinical label fits this plaque best?",
     answer:"Leukoplakia", wrong:["Leukoedema","Pseudomembranous candidiasis","Linea alba"],
     explain:"A white plaque that does not wipe off, does not fade on stretching and has no removable cause is leukoplakia, a diagnosis of exclusion. Pseudomembranous candidiasis wipes off; leukoedema fades on stretching.",
     node:"m10-n01" },
   { q:"Which biopsy is appropriate?",
     answer:"Incisional, from the most suspicious area", wrong:["Excisional, with a 2 to 3 mm margin of normal tissue","Brush cytology first, then biopsy only if abnormal","Toluidine blue staining, then review in 6 months"],
     explain:"Large lesions, or anything suspected to be malignant, need an incisional biopsy of the most suspicious area with some adjacent normal tissue. Brush cytology and toluidine blue cannot replace a scalpel biopsy.",
     node:"m10-n12" },
   { q:"How should the specimen be handled?",
     answer:"Lift it on a suture and fix it in 10% buffered formalin", wrong:["Grip it with toothed forceps and send it in saline","Cut the margins with electrosurgery to limit the bleeding","Place it in Michel's medium for routine histology"],
     explain:"A suture lifts the tissue without crush artefact and can mark orientation. Fix at once in 10% neutral-buffered formalin at no less than about 10 times the specimen volume. Michel's medium is for direct immunofluorescence.",
     node:"m21-n09" },
   { reveal:"<p>The report describes dysplasia extending into the middle third of the epithelium.</p>",
     q:"What is the grade, and what follows?",
     answer:"Moderate dysplasia: complete removal and follow-up", wrong:["Mild dysplasia: review once in a year, then discharge","Severe dysplasia: radiotherapy at once","Carcinoma in situ: no further action"],
     explain:"Dysplasia in the lower third is mild, the middle third moderate, beyond that severe, and full thickness carcinoma in situ. Moderate or severe dysplasia is removed completely, with long-term follow-up.",
     node:"m10-n02" },
   { q:"Why must his whole mouth be followed for life after excision?",
     answer:"Field cancerisation can produce new primaries", wrong:["Excised dysplasia always regrows at the scar","Dysplastic cells spread through the blood","The biopsy seeds tumour along the incision"],
     explain:"Because tobacco exposes the whole mucosa, patients may develop multiple primary tumours: field cancerisation. Excision of one lesion does not end the risk.",
     node:"m05-n05" },
   { reveal:"<p>He says he is not ready to stop smoking.</p>",
     q:"Which approach fits a patient unwilling to quit?",
     answer:"The 5Rs: relevance, risks, rewards, roadblocks, repetition", wrong:["Setting a firm quit date for him next week","Refusing any further dental care until he has stopped smoking","Prescribing varenicline at once, without any discussion"],
     explain:"For the unwilling patient the 5Rs build motivation. Arguing provokes resistance, and a plan he has not chosen fails.",
     node:"m14-n10" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Oral medicine named the lesion by exclusion, and the biopsy rules decided how to get tissue the pathologist can read. General pathology explained the grading and why risk persists across the whole field. Health-promotion counselling then addressed the cause that drives every step.</p>" },

{ id:"c07", title:"Dry mouth and root caries in an older adult", areas:["OPG","BIO"],
  stem:"<p>A 79-year-old woman takes amitriptyline, oxybutynin, hydrochlorothiazide and ramipril, and buys diphenhydramine for sleep. She complains of a dry mouth. The mirror sticks to her cheek and there is no saliva pool in the floor of the mouth. Several root surfaces of her lower canines and premolars have soft, yellow-brown, plaque-covered lesions at the gingival margin.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Which receptor do most of her drying drugs block?",
     answer:"Muscarinic M3 receptors on acinar cells", wrong:["Beta-1 receptors on striated duct cells","Nicotinic receptors in the otic ganglion","Alpha-1 receptors on myoepithelial cells"],
     explain:"Watery salivary flow depends on acetylcholine at muscarinic M3 receptors on acinar cells. Tricyclics, first-generation antihistamines and bladder antimuscarinics block them, and the effects add up across drugs.",
     node:"m24-n02" },
   { q:"Why does losing stimulated flow raise her caries risk so sharply?",
     answer:"Stimulated saliva carries much more bicarbonate", wrong:["Stimulated saliva carries much less calcium and phosphate","Resting saliva is by far the main buffer against acid","Stimulated saliva has more sodium removed"],
     explain:"At high flow the fluid spends less time in the ducts, so less sodium is reabsorbed and more bicarbonate is added. Stimulated saliva therefore buffers plaque acid far better than resting saliva.",
     node:"m03-n08" },
   { q:"Why do her roots decay when her enamel does not?",
     answer:"Root critical pH is about 6.2 to 6.7", wrong:["Root critical pH is about 4.5","Cementum has more mineral than enamel","Fluoride cannot bind to root surfaces"],
     explain:"Cementum and root dentine have smaller, more carbonated crystals and far more organic matrix, so they dissolve at a higher pH than enamel (about 5.5). Fluorapatite's critical pH is about 4.5.",
     node:"m08-n02" },
   { q:"Which home fluoride product best arrests her lesions?",
     answer:"5000 ppm fluoride toothpaste daily", wrong:["1.23% APF gel in trays daily","Fluoride tablets dissolved daily","1000 ppm toothpaste, then a rinse"],
     explain:"High-fluoride toothpaste (1.1% NaF, 5000 ppm) daily is the home measure for high risk, with 5% NaF varnish every 3 to 6 months in the office. APF gel is a professional product, and rinsing after brushing washes fluoride away.",
     node:"m08-n02" },
   { reveal:"<p>One lesion on 4.4 is cavitated and cannot be kept clean, and the site is hard to keep dry.</p>",
     q:"Which material suits the restoration?",
     answer:"Glass ionomer", wrong:["Bonded resin composite","Silver amalgam","Zinc phosphate"],
     explain:"Glass ionomer bonds chemically and releases fluoride, and suits Class V and root caries, especially where moisture control is poor. Composite bonding needs a dry field.",
     node:"m08-n06" },
   { q:"What is your role regarding her medicines?",
     answer:"Ask her physician about less anticholinergic options", wrong:["Stop the oxybutynin and tell her physician later","Add pilocarpine and continue every current drug","Tell her to halve the dose of every drug that dries the mouth"],
     explain:"The dentist does not stop a medical drug. Ask the physician whether a less anticholinergic alternative exists; the Beers Criteria list strong anticholinergics as drugs to avoid in older adults.",
     node:"m24-n02" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Pharmacology identified the cause (anticholinergic burden at M3 receptors), physiology explained the lost protection (bicarbonate in stimulated saliva) and cariology explained why roots fail first (a higher critical pH). Management worked from arrest to restoration to the medicines, with the physician changing the drugs.</p>" },

{ id:"c08", title:"Acute pulpitis in a pregnant patient", areas:["END","PHA","OMR"],
  stem:"<p>A 31-year-old woman, 32 weeks pregnant, has had two nights of spontaneous throbbing pain from the lower left. Cold on 3.6 causes pain that lingers for over a minute. 3.6 has deep occlusal caries and is not tender to percussion or palpation. She wants to wait until after the baby is born.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"What is the endodontic diagnosis of 3.6?",
     answer:"Symptomatic irreversible pulpitis, normal apical tissues", wrong:["Reversible pulpitis, symptomatic apical periodontitis","Pulp necrosis, asymptomatic apical periodontitis","Symptomatic irreversible pulpitis, chronic apical abscess"],
     explain:"Lingering thermal pain and spontaneous pain mark symptomatic irreversible pulpitis. No pain on percussion or palpation means normal apical tissues.",
     node:"m20-n01" },
   { q:"When should 3.6 be treated?",
     answer:"Now, because emergencies are treated when they occur", wrong:["After delivery, because the fetus is most at risk now","In the second trimester, which has already passed for her","Only once she has stopped breastfeeding later"],
     explain:"Pregnancy is a reason to plan care, not withhold it. Untreated pain and infection are worse for mother and fetus than careful treatment; only purely elective work is timed for the second trimester.",
     node:"m13-n08" },
   { q:"How do you handle the radiograph in a Canadian office?",
     answer:"Take the periapical needed, with a thyroid shield", wrong:["Defer all radiographs until after delivery","Take it only with a full-length lead apron","Take a panoramic view instead of the periapical film"],
     explain:"Take what diagnosis requires. Safety Code 30 requires a thyroid shield whenever it will not interfere with the image; a lead apron is not required for routine dental imaging.",
     node:"m09-n02" },
   { reveal:"<p>As the chair reclines she becomes pale, nauseated and faint.</p>",
     q:"What is the cause, and what do you do?",
     answer:"Caval compression: tilt her onto her left side", wrong:["Vasovagal syncope: raise her legs while supine","Hypoglycaemia: give 15 g of glucose by mouth","Anaphylaxis: give epinephrine into the thigh"],
     explain:"Late in pregnancy the uterus compresses the inferior vena cava when she lies flat, so venous return and cardiac output fall. Roll her to the left or wedge the right hip.",
     node:"m13-n02" },
   { q:"Which local anaesthetic choice has the most supporting data in pregnancy?",
     answer:"Lidocaine with epinephrine, lowest effective dose", wrong:["Prilocaine plain, as epinephrine is unsafe in pregnancy","Bupivacaine, as it needs the fewest cartridges overall","No anaesthetic at all, to avoid any fetal drug exposure"],
     explain:"Do not withhold necessary care. Lidocaine with epinephrine has the most supporting data; use the lowest effective dose and aspirate.",
     node:"m12-n04" },
   { q:"What relieves her pain at this visit?",
     answer:"Pulpotomy or pulpectomy, with no antibiotic", wrong:["Amoxicillin for 7 days, then treatment","Penicillin V and analgesics, then review","Extraction with a course of clindamycin"],
     explain:"The most inflamed tissue is in the chamber, so pulpotomy or pulpectomy relieves pain in most patients. Antibiotics do not treat irreversible pulpitis; penicillin did not relieve its pain in a placebo-controlled trial.",
     node:"m20-n05" },
   { q:"Which analgesic do you advise at 32 weeks?",
     answer:"Acetaminophen", wrong:["Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Analgesic-dose aspirin"],
     explain:"Acetaminophen is first line in pregnancy. From 20 weeks NSAIDs can reduce fetal kidney output (FDA 2020), and in the third trimester they risk premature closure of the ductus arteriosus.",
     node:"m13-n08" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Endodontic diagnosis set the procedure and ruled out antibiotics. Pregnancy changed how, not whether: treat now, shield the thyroid, tilt her left to free the vena cava, use lidocaine with epinephrine and choose acetaminophen. The wrong answer at almost every step was to wait.</p>" },

{ id:"c09", title:"Extraction in a patient on a bisphosphonate", areas:["PHA","OSX","EBD"],
  stem:"<p>A 72-year-old woman has taken oral alendronate weekly for 6 years for osteoporosis. 4.6 has fractured below the gingiva and cannot be restored. She has never had radiotherapy to the head and neck. She has heard that her pills can make the jaw rot and is frightened.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"How does alendronate make jaw wounds slow to heal?",
     answer:"It binds bone mineral and poisons osteoclasts", wrong:["It blocks RANKL so osteoclasts never form","It destroys the vessels that feed the socket","It kills osteoblasts at the extraction site"],
     explain:"Bisphosphonates bind bone mineral, poison osteoclasts and stay in bone for years, so socket walls are not remodelled. RANKL blockade is denosumab's mechanism.",
     node:"m11-n08" },
   { q:"What risk of MRONJ do you quote for this extraction?",
     answer:"Well under 1%", wrong:["About 1.6 to 14.8%","Above 20%","About 50%"],
     explain:"In osteoporosis, the risk after an extraction stays well under 1%. The 1.6 to 14.8% figure applies to cancer patients on bisphosphonates. Her more than 4 years of use was treated as higher risk by AAOMS 2014, but it remains small.",
     node:"m21-n05" },
   { q:"What makes her consent to the extraction valid?",
     answer:"She is told the small risk and the alternatives", wrong:["She signs the practice's standard printed consent form","Her daughter agrees to it on her behalf in writing","She agrees after the extraction has begun"],
     explain:"Valid consent relates to the treatment, is informed, is voluntary and is not obtained by misrepresentation. A signed form is evidence of consent, not consent itself, and a capable adult consents for herself.",
     node:"m14-n07" },
   { q:"She asks whether to stop the alendronate first. What do you say?",
     answer:"Any drug holiday is her prescriber's decision", wrong:["Stop it for 3 months before the extraction","Stop it for good, since its effect on bone is over","Double it after surgery to protect the bone"],
     explain:"Evidence for drug holidays is limited, and stopping is the prescriber's decision, never the dentist's. For osteoporosis doses, needed surgery usually proceeds with consent.",
     node:"m21-n05" },
   { q:"Which surgical approach lowers her risk?",
     answer:"Atraumatic, minimal flap, smooth bone, primary closure", wrong:["A wide flap with generous bone removal for good access","Open socket packed with a dressing and left to heal alone","Rapid elevation, with the bone shaved back until it bleeds"],
     explain:"Use atraumatic technique, minimal flaps, smooth sharp bone, primary closure where possible and chlorhexidine rinses, then review until healed.",
     node:"m21-n05" },
   { reveal:"<p>At 10 weeks a small area of bone remains exposed in the socket. It is painless, with no sign of infection.</p>",
     q:"What is the diagnosis and stage?",
     answer:"MRONJ stage 1", wrong:["MRONJ stage 0","MRONJ stage 2","Osteoradionecrosis"],
     explain:"She meets all three criteria: antiresorptive therapy, bone exposed for more than 8 weeks, and no radiation to the jaws. Exposed bone without symptoms or infection is stage 1, managed with an antimicrobial rinse and review.",
     node:"m13-n10" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Pharmacology explained the risk (osteoclasts switched off), and surgery sized it by indication: osteoporosis doses keep the risk well under 1%. Ethics turned that number into informed consent, and the prescriber, not the dentist, owns the drug. The AAOMS definition then staged the complication.</p>" },

{ id:"c10", title:"Extraction in a patient with haemophilia A", areas:["BIO","PHA","OSX"],
  stem:"<p>A 24-year-old man has haemophilia A with a factor VIII level of 3%. 3.6 is non-restorable and must be extracted. As a teenager he bled from a socket for two days, starting several hours after an extraction that had seemed fine at first. He takes no regular medicines.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Which laboratory pattern fits haemophilia A?",
     answer:"Long aPTT, normal PT and platelet count", wrong:["Long PT, normal aPTT and platelet count","Low platelet count, normal PT and aPTT","Long PT and aPTT, low platelet count"],
     explain:"Factor VIII sits in the intrinsic path, which the aPTT reports. PT and the platelet count are normal.",
     node:"m03-n06" },
   { q:"How severe is his haemophilia?",
     answer:"Moderate", wrong:["Severe","Mild","Subclinical"],
     explain:"Severity follows factor level: severe below 1%, moderate 1 to 5%, mild 5 to 40%. At 3% he is moderate.",
     node:"m13-n07" },
   { q:"Why did his old socket start bleeding hours after the extraction?",
     answer:"The platelet plug formed but fibrin never reinforced it", wrong:["The platelet plug failed to form in the first minutes","The vasoconstrictor wore off and caused a rebound in pressure","An infection had eroded a vessel in the socket wall by then"],
     explain:"A clotting-factor problem gives delayed bleeding: primary haemostasis works, but without enough thrombin the plug is never stabilised by fibrin. Platelet problems bleed immediately.",
     node:"m03-n06" },
   { q:"Which injection must wait until factor replacement is given?",
     answer:"Inferior alveolar nerve block", wrong:["Buccal infiltration","Intrapapillary injection","Intraligamentary injection"],
     explain:"An inferior alveolar block or lingual infiltration can bleed into the pterygoid space and threaten the airway. Buccal infiltration, intrapapillary and intraligamentary injections need no factor cover.",
     node:"m13-n07" },
   { q:"The haematologist mentions desmopressin. Which statement is correct?",
     answer:"It helps mild haemophilia A, not haemophilia B", wrong:["It helps only haemophilia B, never haemophilia A","It replaces the missing factor IX directly in the plasma","It works in every patient, so no trial dose is needed"],
     explain:"Desmopressin releases stored factor VIII and von Willebrand factor, so it helps mild (and some moderate) haemophilia A and type 1 von Willebrand disease, not haemophilia B. Response is tested first because not everyone responds.",
     node:"m13-n07" },
   { q:"Which local measure counters the fibrinolytic activity of saliva?",
     answer:"Tranexamic acid as a 5% mouthwash", wrong:["Chlorhexidine as a 0.12% mouthwash","Warm saline mouthwashes every hour","Hydrogen peroxide on soaked gauze"],
     explain:"Saliva is rich in fibrinolytic activity. Tranexamic acid blocks plasmin activation, so the clot is not dissolved; use it with pressure, a haemostatic agent and sutures.",
     node:"m21-n03" },
   { q:"Which analgesic do you prescribe?",
     answer:"Acetaminophen", wrong:["Aspirin","Ibuprofen","Naproxen"],
     explain:"Use acetaminophen and avoid aspirin, which blocks platelets irreversibly. An NSAID is given only if the haematologist agrees.",
     node:"m11-n04" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Physiology identified the failed stage (secondary haemostasis, shown by a long aPTT and delayed bleeding). Medicine replaced it with the haematologist, anatomy decided which injection needed cover, and local measures and drug choice protected the clot from saliva and from platelet blockers.</p>" },

{ id:"c11", title:"Cleft lip and palate through the mixed dentition", areas:["BIO","OPG"],
  stem:"<p>An 8-year-old girl was born with a unilateral left cleft of the lip, alveolus and palate. Her lip and palate were repaired in infancy. She now has a left posterior crossbite, an anterior crossbite and a Class III tendency. 2.2 is missing and the 2.3 crown is still high in the bone beside the cleft.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Her cleft lip arose from failure of which two structures to fuse?",
     answer:"Maxillary and medial nasal prominences", wrong:["Lateral and medial nasal prominences","Two mandibular prominences in the midline","Frontonasal and mandibular prominences"],
     explain:"Cleft lip is failure of the maxillary prominence to fuse with the medial nasal prominence at about week 6 to 7. It may run through the alveolus between lateral incisor and canine.",
     node:"m06-n06" },
   { q:"Why can a cleft lip occur without a cleft palate?",
     answer:"They form at different times from different tissues", wrong:["The palate always closes before the lip forms","Only the lip is built from the first pharyngeal arch","Palatal shelves fuse back to front, so they never fail"],
     explain:"The lip forms at about week 6 to 7; the palatal shelves elevate at about weeks 7 to 8 and fuse front to back from about week 9, completing by about week 12. Separate events can fail separately.",
     node:"m06-n06" },
   { q:"At what ages were her repairs most likely done?",
     answer:"Lip at about 3 months, palate at 9 to 18 months", wrong:["Lip at birth and palate at about 5 years of age","Lip at about 3 months, palate at 8 to 11 years","Lip and palate together at about 4 years old"],
     explain:"The common sequence is lip repair at about 3 months and palate repair at about 9 to 18 months, so speech can develop. The 8 to 11 year window is for the alveolar bone graft.",
     node:"m22-n07" },
   { q:"Why does she have a Class III tendency?",
     answer:"Palatal repair scar restricts maxillary growth", wrong:["The cleft makes the mandible overgrow forward","A thumb habit has pushed the maxilla backward","Missing 2.2 lets the lower arch drift forward"],
     explain:"Scar tissue from the palate repair restricts maxillary growth, which also explains her crossbites. The mandible is not primarily at fault.",
     node:"m22-n07" },
   { q:"Why is the alveolar bone graft planned now rather than at 14?",
     answer:"2.3 erupts at about 11 to 12 and needs bone", wrong:["The graft must precede the permanent first molar","Bone grafts fail once the second molars erupt","The midpalatal suture fuses at about age 9"],
     explain:"The graft is placed at about 8 to 11 years, before the canine erupts through the cleft. The maxillary canine erupts at about 11 to 12, late because it travels furthest.",
     node:"m06-n08" },
   { q:"An implant is planned for the missing 2.2 once she is grown. What happens to the space meanwhile?",
     answer:"Keep it open and hold it until growth ends", wrong:["Close it now with the canine moved mesially","Leave it alone and reassess only at age 18","Close it with a resin-bonded bridge at age 9"],
     explain:"With an implant planned for a missing upper lateral, open or keep the space, guide the canine distally into its own place and hold the space until growth ends.",
     node:"m22-n04" },
   { q:"Which professional fluoride measure suits her high caries risk?",
     answer:"5% NaF varnish every 3 to 6 months", wrong:["1.23% APF gel in trays every month","Fluoride tablets daily from now on","38% silver diamine fluoride on all sound teeth"],
     explain:"For children at elevated risk, the core plan is twice-daily fluoride toothpaste, professional fluoride varnish every 3 to 6 months, sealants on the first permanent molars and diet counselling. Supplements are not recommended for most Canadian children.",
     node:"m23-n02" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Embryology explained what failed and when. Team care then followed growth: lip and palate early for speech, the bone graft timed to the canine's eruption, and implant space held until growth ends. The scarred maxilla explained the crossbites and Class III tendency, and prevention ran alongside throughout.</p>" },

{ id:"c12", title:"Sedation for an anxious adult", areas:["PHA","BIO","EBD"],
  stem:"<p>A 38-year-old healthy woman has avoided dental care for 10 years after a painful extraction. Her Modified Dental Anxiety Scale score is 22. She needs several restorations and is ashamed of her teeth. She asks to be 'put to sleep' for everything.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"What does her MDAS score of 22 indicate?",
     answer:"High dental anxiety", wrong:["Low dental anxiety","Normal, mild apprehension","A specific needle phobia"],
     explain:"The MDAS runs from 5 to 25, and 19 or more marks high dental anxiety, a patient likely to need extra time or special management.",
     node:"m11-n07" },
   { reveal:"<p>As the first visit begins she becomes light-headed and breathes fast and deep, with tingling around her mouth and in her fingers. Her saturation is 99%.</p>",
     q:"What is happening, and what do you do?",
     answer:"Hyperventilation: sit her up, coach slow breathing", wrong:["Asthma: give salbutamol by spacer, one puff at a time","Hypoglycaemia: give 15 g of glucose by mouth","Anaphylaxis: give epinephrine into the thigh"],
     explain:"Rapid deep breathing blows off CO2, and the alkalosis lowers ionised calcium, so the mouth and fingers tingle. Saturation is normal or high, so oxygen does nothing: stop, sit her upright, reassure her and coach slow breathing.",
     node:"m03-n02" },
   { q:"Alongside a behavioural plan, which sedation do you start with?",
     answer:"Nitrous oxide and oxygen", wrong:["General anaesthesia in hospital","Intravenous sedation","High-dose oral diazepam"],
     explain:"Start with the least that works: nitrous oxide, then an oral benzodiazepine, then IV sedation or general anaesthesia. Nitrous oxide comes closest to the ideal sedative.",
     node:"m11-n07" },
   { q:"Why does nitrous oxide act and wear off so quickly?",
     answer:"It has low blood:gas solubility", wrong:["It has high blood:gas solubility","It is metabolised fast by the liver","It binds tightly to haemoglobin"],
     explain:"A gas with low blood:gas solubility saturates blood quickly, so alveolar and brain levels rise fast and fall fast. Nitrous oxide is not metabolised; it leaves through the lungs, which is also why 100% oxygen is given for at least 5 minutes at the end, to prevent diffusion hypoxia.",
     node:"m11-n10" },
   { reveal:"<p>For a longer visit, oral triazolam is considered. She is currently taking clarithromycin for a chest infection.</p>",
     q:"What is the concern?",
     answer:"Clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4 and deepens sedation", wrong:["Clarithromycin induces CYP3A4 and so blocks the sedation","Triazolam inactivates the clarithromycin in the gut wall","The pair cause serotonin toxicity within hours"],
     explain:"Triazolam is cleared by CYP3A4. Erythromycin, clarithromycin or an azole inhibit it, so levels rise and sedation deepens.",
     node:"m11-n08" },
   { reveal:"<p>At a later visit under oral benzodiazepine sedation she responds only to repeated painful stimulation, and her breathing is slow.</p>",
     q:"After supporting her airway, what reverses the benzodiazepine?",
     answer:"Flumazenil 0.2 mg IV over 15 seconds", wrong:["Naloxone IV, repeated each minute","Glucagon 1 mg intramuscularly","Epinephrine 0.5 mg intramuscularly"],
     explain:"She is deeply sedated. Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine site: 0.2 mg IV over 15 seconds, then small doses at about 1-minute intervals to a usual maximum of 1 mg. Naloxone reverses opioids, not benzodiazepines.",
     node:"m11-n07" },
   { reveal:"<p>Your assistant, who works daily beside nitrous oxide, reports numb feet.</p>",
     q:"Which mechanism explains her symptom?",
     answer:"Nitrous oxide oxidises vitamin B12", wrong:["Nitrous oxide chelates serum calcium","Nitrous oxide blocks sodium channels","Nitrous oxide inhibits cholinesterase"],
     explain:"Nitrous oxide oxidises vitamin B12 and so inhibits methionine synthase; chronic exposure causes neuropathy. Scavenging, a fitted hood and ventilation keep exposure under the NIOSH limit of 25 ppm.",
     node:"m14-n02" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Anxiety assessment set the need, and respiratory physiology separated a hyperventilation episode from the emergencies it mimics. Pharmacology set the sedation ladder, starting with nitrous oxide because its low solubility makes it fast and controllable. Drug interactions and the sedation continuum decided safety and rescue, and occupational health protected the team from the same gas.</p>" },

{ id:"c13", title:"A medically complex patient for complete dentures", areas:["BIO","RPI","PHA"],
  stem:"<p>A 76-year-old woman on warfarin, who also takes oxybutynin for an overactive bladder, wants new complete dentures. Her present set is 15 years old and loose. Her lower face looks collapsed and both corners of her mouth are red and cracked. The palate under the upper denture is red all over, but only where the denture covers it, and it is painless. She sleeps in her dentures.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Which denture fault most likely contributes to her cracked corners?",
     answer:"Reduced occlusal vertical dimension", wrong:["Excessive occlusal vertical dimension","Over-extended lower lingual flange","Teeth set too far to the buccal"],
     explain:"Angular cheilitis is Candida, often with Staphylococcus aureus, favoured by a reduced vertical dimension that pools saliva in the folds. A collapsed lower face fits the same fault.",
     node:"m04-n09" },
   { q:"How is the palatal finding classified?",
     answer:"Newton class II denture stomatitis", wrong:["Newton class I denture stomatitis","Newton class III denture stomatitis","Erythroplakia of the hard palate"],
     explain:"Newton class I is pinpoint redness, class II diffuse redness confined to the denture area, and class III granular or papillary hyperplasia.",
     node:"m17-n04" },
   { q:"Which antifungal plan is safest for her?",
     answer:"Topical nystatin, with dentures out at night", wrong:["Miconazole oral gel, with dentures out at night","Systemic fluconazole, with dentures out at night","Topical nystatin, with dentures worn at night"],
     explain:"Miconazole and fluconazole raise warfarin's effect. Nystatin treats the mucosa, and the denture must also be cleaned and left out at night, or it reinfects the mouth.",
     node:"m11-n08" },
   { q:"Why does her dry mouth from oxybutynin worsen denture retention?",
     answer:"Less saliva leaves less mucin to lubricate and seal", wrong:["More saliva builds up and floats the denture off the palate","Oxybutynin softens the acrylic of the denture base over time","Dry mucosa thickens quickly and lifts the denture off the ridge"],
     explain:"Oxybutynin blocks the muscarinic drive to the glands. Saliva's mucins lubricate speech, chewing, swallowing and denture retention, so hyposalivation loosens dentures.",
     node:"m03-n08" },
   { q:"What comes before the new impressions?",
     answer:"Rest the tissues and use a tissue conditioner", wrong:["Take the final impressions at once to save a visit","Reline the old dentures with hard chairside acrylic","Rebase the old dentures to restore the OVD"],
     explain:"Inflamed or distorted tissue is first rested and treated with a tissue conditioner, replaced every few days, because an impression records whatever distortion is present.",
     node:"m17-n04" },
   { q:"Her rest vertical dimension measures 70 mm. Which occlusal vertical dimension fits?",
     answer:"66 to 68 mm", wrong:["70 to 72 mm","62 to 64 mm","72 to 74 mm"],
     explain:"OVD equals rest vertical dimension minus the freeway space, usually 2 to 4 mm at the premolars. An OVD at or above rest removes the freeway space and loads the ridges all day.",
     node:"m17-n02" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>The corners of the mouth pointed to a prosthodontic fault, the collapsed vertical dimension, and microbiology named the organisms. Pharmacology steered the antifungal away from azoles because of warfarin and explained the dry mouth. Prosthodontics then sequenced the work: heal the tissues, then rebuild the vertical dimension with a correct freeway space.</p>" },

{ id:"c14", title:"Staging and treating periodontitis in a diabetic smoker", areas:["PER","PHA","EBD"],
  stem:"<p>A 45-year-old man smokes 15 cigarettes a day and has type 2 diabetes with an HbA1c of 8.2%. His worst interdental attachment loss is 6 mm, bone loss reaches the middle third, and at the worst tooth it is 45% of the root. His deepest pocket is 7 mm and 3.6 has a class II furcation. No teeth have been lost to periodontitis, and more than 30% of teeth are affected.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"What is his stage?",
     answer:"Stage III", wrong:["Stage II","Stage IV","Stage I"],
     explain:"CAL of 5 mm or more with bone loss to the middle third is stage III or IV. Probing depth of 6 mm or more and a class II furcation are stage III complexity factors; stage IV needs complex rehabilitation, which he does not.",
     node:"m19-n03" },
   { q:"What is his grade?",
     answer:"Grade C, raised by smoking and HbA1c", wrong:["Grade B, as bone loss over age is 1.0","Grade A, as he has a heavy biofilm load","No grade: code it as diabetic periodontitis"],
     explain:"45% divided by 45 years is 1.0, which alone is grade B. Smoking 10 or more a day and HbA1c of 7.0% or more each raise it to grade C. Diabetes is a grade modifier, not a separate diagnosis.",
     node:"m19-n03" },
   { q:"What can he expect non-surgical therapy to do to his HbA1c?",
     answer:"Lower it by about 0.4 points at three months", wrong:["Lower it by about 4 points at three months","Raise it briefly for about the first three months","Leave it unchanged at every time point"],
     explain:"The periodontal link runs both ways. Non-surgical therapy lowers HbA1c by about 0.4 points at three months; it does not replace medical care.",
     node:"m13-n06" },
   { reveal:"<p>He says he wants to stop smoking and asks what can help.</p>",
     q:"Which pharmacotherapy options are evidence-based for him?",
     answer:"Patch plus short-acting nicotine, varenicline or bupropion", wrong:["Nicotine gum on its own, used only at the moments cravings peak","Long-term benzodiazepine to ease withdrawal","Nicotine patch alone, kept at the lowest dose throughout"],
     explain:"Medication options are nicotine replacement (a patch plus a short-acting form), varenicline or bupropion, within the 5As. Bupropion is avoided with seizure or eating disorders.",
     node:"m14-n10" },
   { q:"Should systemic antibiotics be added to his subgingival instrumentation?",
     answer:"No, they are not routine for him", wrong:["Yes, amoxicillin for every smoker","Yes, doxycycline 20 mg for six months","Yes, metronidazole instead of scaling"],
     explain:"Systemic antibiotics are not routine; resistance and adverse effects outweigh the small average gain. When used, they go with instrumentation, never instead of it, and the guideline advises against sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.",
     node:"m19-n05" },
   { reveal:"<p>At re-evaluation, 3.6 still has a 6 mm bleeding pocket.</p>",
     q:"What happens to that site?",
     answer:"Step 3: surgical therapy for the residual pocket", wrong:["Step 4: supportive care, as the pocket is now closed","Step 1: repeat oral hygiene instruction and nothing else","No treatment, as a 6 mm depth is within normal limits"],
     explain:"The endpoints are no pocket over 4 mm that bleeds and no pocket of 6 mm or more. Sites of 6 mm or more go to Step 3 surgery.",
     node:"m19-n05" },
   { q:"Which supportive care interval suits him at first?",
     answer:"About 3 months", wrong:["About 12 months","About 6 months","Only when symptoms recur"],
     explain:"The EFP range is 3 to 12 months, tailored to risk. Smoking, poorly controlled diabetes, grade C disease and residual bleeding pockets all shorten the interval.",
     node:"m19-n08" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Staging measured the damage and grading measured the risk, with smoking and HbA1c as the modifiers that pushed him to grade C. Endocrinology and tobacco counselling attacked those modifiers, while the EFP steps ran from cause-related therapy through re-evaluation to surgery and a short maintenance interval.</p>" },

{ id:"c15", title:"A cracked molar through to the crown", areas:["END","RPI","BIO"],
  stem:"<p>A 48-year-old man has had sharp pain when chewing on the lower right for two months, worst when he lets go of hard food. 4.6 has a large MOD amalgam. His partner says he grinds his teeth at night. Cold on 4.6 gives a sharp pain that stops within seconds, and percussion is painless. The radiograph is normal.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Which test best reproduces his complaint?",
     answer:"Bite test on individual cusps", wrong:["Electric pulp test on the buccal","Heat test with warm gutta-percha","Palpation over the root apex"],
     explain:"A cracked tooth hurts on release of biting, and the bite test localises it to one cusp.",
     node:"m20-n02" },
   { q:"With transillumination, a line on the distolingual cusp blocks the light. What does that mean?",
     answer:"A crack, not a craze line", wrong:["A craze line, not a crack","A stain, not a crack","Amalgam shine-through only"],
     explain:"Craze lines are enamel-only, painless and let light pass through. A crack that blocks transmitted light and hurts on release of biting suggests a cracked tooth.",
     node:"m15-n01" },
   { q:"What is the pulp diagnosis now?",
     answer:"Reversible pulpitis", wrong:["Symptomatic irreversible pulpitis","Pulp necrosis","Asymptomatic irreversible pulpitis"],
     explain:"Sharp pain to cold that stops within seconds, with no spontaneous pain, is reversible pulpitis. Lingering or spontaneous pain would mean irreversible pulpitis.",
     node:"m20-n01" },
   { q:"Which restoration protects 4.6?",
     answer:"Cuspal coverage with an onlay or crown", wrong:["An inlay replacing the MOD amalgam","A larger MOD amalgam retained by pins","Occlusal adjustment and no restoration at all"],
     explain:"An inlay sits between the cusps and can wedge them apart. An onlay covers cusps so load compresses the tooth instead of splitting it; choose it when a cusp has cracked.",
     node:"m15-n05" },
   { reveal:"<p>Before the definitive restoration is placed, cold pain begins to linger and wakes him at night. Root canal treatment of 4.6 is completed.</p>",
     q:"How is the root-filled 4.6 built up?",
     answer:"A core held by the pulp chamber, no post", wrong:["A post in the mesial canal to strengthen it","A threaded active post in the distal canal","An intracoronal temporary filling only"],
     explain:"A molar's pulp chamber usually retains a core by itself, so posts are rarely needed. A post does not strengthen the root, mesial roots perforate easily, and threaded posts build high stress.",
     node:"m16-n04" },
   { q:"Where must the crown margin sit relative to the core?",
     answer:"On sound tooth, 1.5 to 2 mm beyond the core", wrong:["On the core itself, at the gingival margin","On sound tooth, 0.5 mm beyond the core","Within the core, 1 mm above the bone crest"],
     explain:"The crown margin must sit on sound tooth at least about 1.5 to 2 mm apical to the core-tooth junction. This ferrule braces the tooth and keeps the leaky junction under the crown.",
     node:"m16-n01" },
   { q:"How do you protect the new crown from his sleep bruxism?",
     answer:"Hard full-coverage stabilisation splint at night", wrong:["Selective grinding to remove all occlusal interferences","Anterior repositioning splint worn full-time for months","Soft partial-coverage bite plane at night"],
     explain:"A hard full-coverage stabilisation splint spreads the load and protects teeth and restorations. Occlusal adjustment does not treat bruxism, a full-time repositioning splint can leave a posterior open bite, and partial coverage lets uncovered teeth over-erupt.",
     node:"m07-n07" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Endodontic tests found the crack and staged the pulp. Operative principles chose cuspal coverage to stop the crack spreading, and fixed prosthodontics restored the root-filled tooth with a chamber-retained core and a true ferrule. Occlusion closed the loop by protecting the crown from the bruxism that started it.</p>" },

{ id:"c16", title:"Jaw muscle or tooth? A facial pain differential", areas:["BIO","OSX","END","PHA"],
  stem:"<p>A 29-year-old woman has had a dull ache in the right lower molars and in front of the right ear for three months. It is worst in the morning and when chewing, and she clenches under stress. 4.6 and 4.7 respond normally to cold, are not tender to percussion and have no caries; the radiographs are normal. Palpating the right masseter reproduces her familiar pain and sends it into the lower molars.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Why can masseter pain be felt in healthy molars?",
     answer:"Afferents converge on shared second-order neurons", wrong:["The masseter nerve also supplies the molar pulps","Muscle inflammation spreads into the alveolar bone","Tooth and muscle share one peripheral receptor"],
     explain:"Many afferents converge on the same second-order cells in the brainstem, so the brain mislocates the source: referred pain.",
     node:"m03-n03" },
   { q:"Which DC/TMD diagnosis fits?",
     answer:"Myofascial pain with referral", wrong:["Local myalgia","Arthralgia","Disc displacement without reduction"],
     explain:"Familiar pain on palpating the muscle that is felt beyond it, here in the teeth, is myofascial pain with referral. Local myalgia stays at the site palpated; arthralgia is pain at the condyle.",
     node:"m21-n08" },
   { q:"What do you do about 4.6 and 4.7?",
     answer:"No dental treatment, as they test normal", wrong:["Root canal treatment of 4.6 to see if it helps","Extraction of 4.7 as the likely source","Occlusal adjustment of both molars"],
     explain:"If testing finds no dental cause, do not perform exploratory root canal treatment. A sound tooth that tests normal is not treated.",
     node:"m20-n05" },
   { reveal:"<p>She also has a painless click on both opening and closing, and opens 45 mm without deviation.</p>",
     q:"What explains the click, and what does it need?",
     answer:"Disc displacement with reduction: reassurance", wrong:["Disc displacement without reduction: refer for surgery","Subluxation past the eminence: reduce it manually now","Degenerative joint disease: CBCT and splint"],
     explain:"When the disc has slipped forward, the condyle jumps back onto it during opening, giving the click. Painless clicking needs reassurance only. A locked disc limits opening below 40 mm; crepitus suggests degenerative disease.",
     node:"m02-n02" },
   { q:"What is the first-line management of her myofascial pain?",
     answer:"Explanation, self-care, short NSAID course, splint", wrong:["Occlusal adjustment to remove all the interferences","Orthodontic treatment to correct the bite","Long-term opioid analgesia at bedtime"],
     explain:"Treatment starts reversible: explanation, self-care (soft diet, heat, avoiding clenching and gum), short NSAID courses, physiotherapy and a stabilisation splint. Irreversible occlusal treatment is not indicated.",
     node:"m21-n08" },
   { reveal:"<p>Six months later the muscle pain persists despite conservative care.</p>",
     q:"Which drug is supported for her chronic myalgia?",
     answer:"Low-dose amitriptyline at night", wrong:["Long-term oxycodone","Long-term diazepam","Carbamazepine twice daily"],
     explain:"For chronic myalgia a low-dose tricyclic at night helps. Opioids are not used long term (tolerance, dependence, hyperalgesia), benzodiazepines are short-term only, and carbamazepine is for trigeminal neuralgia.",
     node:"m21-n08" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Neurophysiology (convergence) explained why a muscle can mimic a toothache, and endodontic testing proved the teeth sound, so nothing was drilled. TMJ anatomy explained the harmless click, DC/TMD named the pain, and management stayed reversible, with a low-dose tricyclic once the pain became chronic.</p>" },

{ id:"c17", title:"An asthma attack during treatment", areas:["BIO","PHA"],
  stem:"<p>A 22-year-old woman has asthma treated with an inhaled corticosteroid and salbutamol as needed. She has nasal polyps, and aspirin once made her wheezy. During a restoration on 2.5 under articaine 4% with epinephrine 1:100,000, she begins to wheeze, with a prolonged expiration. She can speak in phrases, sits upright and is not agitated; there is no rash and her pressure is normal.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"Why is her expiration long and wheezy?",
     answer:"Narrowed airways collapse as the chest recoils", wrong:["Her alveoli fill with fluid from the heart","Her diaphragm is paralysed by the anaesthetic","She is blowing off carbon dioxide too fast"],
     explain:"Asthma is reversible airway narrowing. Expiration, normally passive, becomes long and wheezy because narrowed airways collapse as the chest recoils.",
     node:"m03-n02" },
   { q:"Which cartridge constituent is the likeliest trigger?",
     answer:"Sodium metabisulfite", wrong:["Methylparaben","Articaine base","Sodium chloride"],
     explain:"Solutions with a vasoconstrictor contain sodium metabisulfite as its antioxidant, and sulfites can provoke bronchospasm in sulfite-sensitive asthmatics. Methylparaben is no longer included in dental cartridges, and true allergy to the anaesthetic base is very rare.",
     node:"m12-n02" },
   { q:"You judge the attack to be moderate. What do you give first?",
     answer:"Salbutamol 4 to 6 puffs by spacer, one at a time", wrong:["Epinephrine 0.5 mg into the mid-anterolateral thigh","Diphenhydramine from the kit, then observe her","Oxygen alone, with her chair laid flat"],
     explain:"Stop and sit her upright. For a moderate attack, GINA 2026 gives 4 to 6 puffs of salbutamol (100 micrograms per puff) by spacer, one puff at a time, repeated up to 3 times 20 to 30 minutes apart. Epinephrine comes first only if anaphylaxis is also present.",
     node:"m13-n03" },
   { q:"How does salbutamol open her airways?",
     answer:"It activates beta-2 receptors on bronchial muscle", wrong:["It blocks beta-2 receptors on bronchial muscle","It activates alpha-1 receptors in the bronchi","It blocks muscarinic receptors on the airway mast cells"],
     explain:"Beta-2 stimulation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. Propranolol, a non-selective blocker, removes that effect.",
     node:"m11-n03" },
   { reveal:"<p>Her saturation is 90%.</p>",
     q:"What saturation do you aim for with oxygen?",
     answer:"92 to 95 percent", wrong:["88 to 92 percent","98 to 100 percent","85 to 88 percent"],
     explain:"In asthma give oxygen if saturation is low, aiming for 92 to 95 percent. The 88 to 92 percent target is for COPD patients who retain carbon dioxide.",
     node:"m13-n03" },
   { reveal:"<p>She recovers and later needs an extraction.</p>",
     q:"Which analgesic do you prescribe, and why?",
     answer:"Acetaminophen, as COX blockade shifts to leukotrienes", wrong:["Ibuprofen, as it relaxes the bronchial smooth muscle directly","Aspirin, as it reduces mast-cell release in the airway","Naproxen, as it spares the airway COX enzyme"],
     explain:"In aspirin-sensitive asthma, often with nasal polyps, blocked COX shifts arachidonic acid toward leukotrienes, which can trigger bronchospasm. Use acetaminophen.",
     node:"m11-n04" },
   { reveal:"<p>At review, creamy white plaques on her soft palate wipe off to leave a red base.</p>",
     q:"What is the likeliest cause, and the advice?",
     answer:"Inhaled-steroid thrush: rinse after each use", wrong:["Lichen planus: topical steroid at night","Leukoplakia: incisional biopsy this week","Frictional keratosis: smooth the cusp"],
     explain:"Plaques that wipe off to a red base are pseudomembranous candidiasis, favoured by steroid inhalers. Treat it and advise rinsing after inhaled steroids.",
     node:"m04-n09" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Respiratory physiology explained the wheeze, and the cartridge's sulfite was the likely trigger. The emergency algorithm chose a bronchodilator first, and autonomic pharmacology explained how it works. Her asthma then governed later choices: acetaminophen instead of NSAIDs, and rinsing to prevent steroid-induced thrush.</p>" },

{ id:"c18", title:"A needlestick injury to a dental assistant", areas:["EBD","BIO","PHA"],
  stem:"<p>A 26-year-old dental assistant pierces her finger while recapping a used needle two-handed after an inferior alveolar block. The source patient's blood-borne virus status is unknown. The assistant completed the hepatitis B series, and her anti-HBs measured afterwards was 120 IU/L.</p>",
  steps:[
   { q:"What is her immediate first aid?",
     answer:"Let it bleed briefly, then wash with soap and water", wrong:["Squeeze it hard, then soak the finger in bleach","Scrub it with a brush and alcohol until it stings","Cover it with a dressing and finish the patient"],
     explain:"Let the wound bleed briefly and freely, then wash with soap and water. Do not apply bleach or scrub.",
     node:"m14-n02" },
   { q:"Which blood-borne virus carries the highest risk after a needlestick from a positive source in a non-immune worker?",
     answer:"Hepatitis B, about 6 to 30%", wrong:["HIV, about 6 to 30%","Hepatitis C, about 6 to 30%","HIV and hepatitis C equally, about 1.8%"],
     explain:"HBV is by far the most transmissible: 6 to 30% in the non-immune, depending mainly on the source's e-antigen. HCV is classically about 1.8% and HIV about 0.3%.",
     node:"m04-n08" },
   { q:"How is the source patient's status established?",
     answer:"The dentist seeks his informed consent to testing", wrong:["The lab tests his stored blood sample without asking him","The assistant asks him directly for a sample","No testing is done; the worker is simply treated for all three"],
     explain:"The dentist reports, assesses the source patient's history and seeks informed consent for testing, often through the patient's physician. Testing cannot simply be done without consent.",
     node:"m14-n02" },
   { q:"What hepatitis B action does she need?",
     answer:"None, as she is a documented responder", wrong:["Hepatitis B immunoglobulin within 48 hours","A second full vaccine series, starting today","A booster dose followed by a new antibody test"],
     explain:"An anti-HBs of at least 10 IU/L after the series means protection, and a documented responder needs no action after exposure.",
     node:"m04-n12" },
   { reveal:"<p>The source patient consents. He is HBsAg-negative and HIV-negative, but positive for hepatitis C antibody and RNA.</p>",
     q:"What follows for the assistant?",
     answer:"Follow-up testing, as HCV has no PEP", wrong:["HCV immunoglobulin within 72 hours","Hepatitis C vaccine series at once","28 days of antiretroviral PEP"],
     explain:"HCV has no vaccine and no post-exposure prophylaxis, but it is curable with direct-acting antivirals, so follow-up testing leads to early treatment.",
     node:"m04-n08" },
   { reveal:"<p>The source patient has cirrhosis from chronic hepatitis C and needs two extractions.</p>",
     q:"What do you need before his surgery?",
     answer:"A complete blood count and INR", wrong:["A double-gloved team only for him","The last appointment of the day","A sterile room kept for his care"],
     explain:"Cirrhosis lowers clotting factors and platelets and increases fibrinolysis. Get a CBC and INR and apply the same limits: INR at or below 3.5, platelets at least 50 x 10⁹/L. Standard precautions already protect the team, whatever the patient's status.",
     node:"m13-n07" },
   { q:"Which practice change prevents a repeat of this injury?",
     answer:"One-handed scoop recapping or a device", wrong:["Two-handed recapping with thicker gloves","Bending the needle before recapping it","Leaving the needle uncapped on the tray"],
     explain:"Most injuries come from needles, burs and instrument handling. Recap one-handed by the scoop technique or with a device, and use puncture-proof containers.",
     node:"m14-n02" }
  ],
  summary:"<p>Infection control set the first minutes: first aid, report, consent-based source testing. Virology ranked the risks and decided each pathway (no action for a vaccine responder, follow-up rather than PEP for HCV), and medicine turned the source's liver disease into a surgical plan. Prevention closed the loop at the recapping step that caused it.</p>" }

]);
